{# The #quiz fragment: prompt, the thing to match, and the candidate buttons.

   The action is in the URL -- `/answer/<qid>/<index>` -- not in a signal. The qid is the
   server's question nonce, so a second click, a stale tab or a replayed request scores
   nothing: the server compares the qid and answers 204. That replaces the panel app's
   "multiple clicks occurred too quickly before server disabled the buttons" guard.

   `data-indicator` sets that local signal while the request is in flight, and every button reads
   it to disable itself -- the visual half of the same protection, done without a round trip.
   It is written in the *value* form, not as `data-indicator:_answering`: attribute keys go
   through `kebab`, which turns a leading underscore into a dash and then drops it, so the key
   form would name the signal `Answering` -- losing the underscore that keeps it local, and
   uploading it with every request.

   These are BUTTONS, not a radio group: a click commits immediately, with no separate submit, so
   button semantics are the honest ones. What they get instead is `role="group"` with a label, and
   a digit accelerator each -- the choices are numbered, so 1-9 answer without the mouse.

   ONE window keydown handler, on the group, mapping the digit to an index -- not one per button.
   Five identical `__window` listeners were five registrations and five teardowns per patch, and
   five copies of the same guard to keep in step.

   The guard is three things:
     - `$_answering` -- an answer already in flight. NOT cosmetic and not removable: the server
       mutates before it streams, so by the time the toasts are playing the *next* question is
       already the live one, and a keypress here would answer a question the player has not been
       shown yet. `aria-busy` says so, and `disabled` on the buttons shows it.
     - `closest(TYPING_TARGETS)` -- a keystroke aimed at a control that has a use for it belongs
       to that control. This was a tagName check on `evt.target`, which only sees the exact element
       the event fired on; `closest` also covers a focused control inside a wrapper (Bulma's
       `<div class="select">`) and anything typed into a rich field. The `?.` matters because
       `evt.target` can be `window` itself.
       The selector is NOT "any form control" -- see `render.TYPING_TARGETS`. It was, and a focused
       difficulty slider (which keeps focus on purpose, or you could not arrow it) then killed 1-9
       for the rest of the session. A range has no use for a digit; a text box does.
     - the digit is in range for THIS question's choice count.
   `evt.key` compared through `Number` rather than as a string, so a ten-choice question would not
   silently accept "1" as ten. #}
<h2 class="intro">{{ intro }}</h2>
<p class="answer">&ldquo;<strong>{{ answer | suits }}</strong>&rdquo;</p>
{# `data-attr` in the OBJECT form, because a hyphenated attribute name cannot survive as a key:
   `data-attr:aria-busy` is kebab-then-camel converted to `ariaBusy` and nothing reaches the DOM --
   no `aria-busy`, no error, the attribute simply never appears. Same family as the `data-bind:`
   naming trap; the object form keeps the literal name.

   The value is the STRING "true"/"false", not the boolean: a boolean `true` renders as `aria-busy=""`
   (datastar's "set the attribute" form), and an empty string is not a valid ARIA state -- it reads
   as neither busy nor idle. ARIA states are enumerated strings, so they are stated as strings. #}
{# `data-indicator` is per REQUESTING ELEMENT: it sets the signal for requests this element starts.
   The buttons have their own for clicks, and the group needs its own for the digit path -- without
   it a keyboard answer left `_answering` false, so the buttons never greyed out and the guard below
   could never be true. Which means two quick digits could both post, and the second would answer
   the NEXT question (already live server-side) before it had been shown. #}
<div class="candidates" role="group" aria-label="answer choices"
     data-indicator="_answering"
     data-attr="{'aria-busy': $_answering ? 'true' : 'false'}"
     data-on:keydown__window="!$_answering && !$_topicsOpen
       && !evt.target.closest?.('{{ TYPING_TARGETS }}')
       && Number(evt.key) >= 1 && Number(evt.key) <= {{ candidates | length }}
       {# the variant goes on the END, after the index this expression appends -- see the note on
          `render.variant_query`; every action URL carries it, and here it is a third fragment #}
       && @post('{{ prefix }}/answer/{{ qid }}/' + (Number(evt.key) - 1) + '{{ variant_query }}')">
  {% for candidate in candidates %}
  <button type="button" class="candidate button"
          data-indicator="_answering"
          data-attr:disabled="$_answering"
          data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/answer/{{ qid }}/{{ loop.index0 }}{{ variant_query }}')">
    <kbd class="accel tag is-light" aria-hidden="true">{{ loop.index }}</kbd>
    <span class="candidate-text">{{ candidate | suits }}</span>
  </button>
  {% endfor %}
</div>
